  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let  $C$ be the event that the selected person pays using a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the customer bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the customer bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the customer bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected customer pays using a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the customers buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the customers buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the customers buy Supreme gasoline, we have \n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the customers that buy Regular gasoline and  $50%$ of the customers that buy Premium gasoline pay with a credit card, we have \n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $57.5%,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(C)=0.575.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(C|S),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\ 0.575 & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+P(C|S)(0.15) \\\\ 0.575 & =0.26+0.225+0.15P(C|S) \\\\ 0.15P(C|S) & =0.09 \\\\ P(C|S) & =0.6=60%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60%$ of Supreme gasoline customers pay with a credit card.",
